// Services split by business capability, each owning its data.
//
//   Orders Service  --[HTTP]->  Payments Service   (sync: needs immediate ack)
//   Orders Service  --[event]-> Inventory Service (async: decoupled in time)
//
// Each service owns its DB (no shared schema) -> no accidental coupling,
//   but a saga is needed if 'reserve + charge' must be all-or-nothing.

// Orders publishes an event; inventory reacts asynchronously.
@RestController
class OrderController {
    @PostMapping("/orders")
    void place(@RequestBody Order o, EventPublisher pub) {
        orders.save(o);
        pub.publish(new OrderPlaced(o.id(), o.items()));   // async -> inventory
    }   // sync call to payments here if immediate confirmation is required.
}

// The distributed cost: if inventory is down, the order is placed but stock
//   isn't reserved. The resolution is eventual consistency + a saga that
//   compensates (cancels the order) if inventory fails — NOT a 2PC/XA
//   transaction across services.

Treat the split decision as a structured audit, not a gut call. A minimal checklist encoded as Java makes the criteria explicit and reviewable in PRs.

```java
public enum SplitDecision { STAY_MONOLITH, MODULAR_MONOLITH, SPLIT }

public record TeamShape(
    int engineers,          // total headcount
    int independentSquads,  // squads with separate release cadence
    boolean ciCdMature,     // can we actually run 10 pipelines?
    boolean observabilityReady  // traces, metrics, on-call
) {}

public record ProductShape(
    int boundedContexts,    // stable >= 6 months
    boolean sloDifferentiation,   // e.g., checkout needs 99.99, analytics 99.5
    boolean scalingSkew,    // one module needs 10x compute of others
    int deployConflictsPerMonth   // teams blocking each other's deploys
) {}

public static SplitDecision decide(TeamShape team, ProductShape product) {
    // Hard floor: no infra maturity = no microservices
    if (!team.ciCdMature() || !team.observabilityReady()) {
        return SplitDecision.STAY_MONOLITH;
    }

    // Below the team threshold, modular monolith captures 80% of the value
    if (team.engineers() < 15 || team.independentSquads() < 3) {
        return SplitDecision.MODULAR_MONOLITH;
    }

    // Pain signals that justify the operational tax
    boolean realPain =
        product.deployConflictsPerMonth() >= 4
        || product.scalingSkew()
        || product.sloDifferentiation();

    boolean boundariesStable = product.boundedContexts() >= 3;

    return (realPain && boundariesStable)
        ? SplitDecision.SPLIT
        : SplitDecision.MODULAR_MONOLITH;
}
```

**Checklist as a team-size vs service-count matrix** — the operational tax budget:

| Engineers | Squads | Recommendation | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| < 10 | 1 | Monolith | coordination is the whole product |
| 10–15 | 1–2 | Modular monolith | boundaries via modules + ArchUnit |
| 15–30 | 2–3 | Modular monolith OR first extraction | extract only if concrete pain |
| 30–80 | 3–6 | Microservices, 5–8 services | one bounded context per squad |
| 80+ | 6+ | Microservices, 10+ services | Conway's law takes over anyway |

**Operational cost budget per service** — 3 to 5 engineer-hours per week (CI/CD, monitoring, alerts, on-call shared). Multiply by N services before committing: a 10-service fleet implies 30–50 weekly hours *just on ops*, before any feature work. If that exceeds 25% of your team's capacity, you're understaffed for the architecture.
